Original commit body from 338e7e1272dac20ddb8c9671721471914815a226:

Reverse-slice class expansion is spec-green across seven container kinds,
but the expansion only does anything where the ANALYZER emitted the
container's members as symbols. Measured on a uniform probe corpus
generated into /tmp: no analyzer that emits an `enum` emits any of its
variants (0 of 5 -- typescript, java, rust, csharp, swift), and
abstract-type members are missing for typescript, rust and swift while
go, java and csharp emit them.

The consumer consequence is not cosmetic:

  slice --entry Color --language rust --reverse   -> 1 node
  slice --entry Color --language java --reverse   -> 2 nodes

so a consumer reads "this enum is dead" or "this trait has no users"
when neither is true.

Root cause is a MISSING NODE, not a missing edge. Neither the slicer nor
the containment linker is at fault -- the linker has handled trait/enum/
struct containers since 1ecae423af (2026-02-08) and roots every container
whose members exist. The new columns therefore measure span nesting
rather than `contains` edges, because this matrix runs one analyzer in
isolation and `contains` is minted downstream.

Two columns rather than one: the families fail independently, and a
merged column would let a language pass on its abstract members while
its enum variants stayed invisible.

The filed framing was wrong on the scope axis. WI-duguk scoped to three
kinds -- trait (1 node), enum (5), module (0, "untestable") -- from a
SELF-ANALYSIS substrate, i.e. hypergumbo run on a Python repo. Module
containment in fact works (ruby 100%; 20+ analyzers emit kind="module");
the zero was INV-hojus collapsing Python module-kind into file-kind two
weeks before that substrate. The real axis is (language, container-kind),
which a per-kind scoping cannot express: scala trait works while rust
trait does not; csharp interface works while typescript interface does
not. Two live holes the filing never looked at -- typescript `interface`
and swift `protocol` -- sit outside its stated scope entirely.

Not by design: no ADR, spec passage or doc excludes enum variants or
member signatures from being symbols, and the niche analyzers already
emit them (verified live: D's `enum Color` yields `field Color.red` /
`field Color.green`, so the containment linker roots them). The coverage
story runs backwards from the usual one -- mainstream is the gap.

Non-vacuity in both directions: 3 hard passes (csharp/go/java
emits_abstract_members) alongside 8 strict xfails, so the check is proven
able to yield a positive. Holes are xfail(strict=True), never imperative
pytest.xfail(), so fixing an analyzer XPASSes its cell and forces the
entry's removal in the same PR.

Scope disclosed: the matrix covers the eight core-availability fixture
languages, so the scala, cpp and kotlin enum gaps this investigation also
measured are real but NOT gated here -- they need grammar wheels the
matrix deliberately does not require. Python and JavaScript are marked
not-applicable with a written re-eval trigger (Python's enum and Protocol
are both classes, already locked by emits_field; JavaScript has neither
construct), as is Go's enum (a `const` block whose members are siblings
of the type, not nested in its body).

The spec keeps its green mark on the slicer mechanism -- which genuinely
expands all seven kinds -- and now names the vacuous cells inline, so the
delivered-mark no longer reads as an outcome guarantee.

Testing note: `test_entrypoint_parity.py` shares this fixture corpus but
smart-test did not select it -- changes under `tests/fixtures/**` are
data, invisible to the import-graph slicer (1045 lines of smart-test,
zero mentions of "fixture"). Ran it by hand: 8 passed. Filed separately
as a fifth route to the test-selection defect class.

